ther cultures but you know  this is about you it is personal yeah  yeah you know it's it's difficult as a  food writer you never really  want to sort of pigeonhole yourself into  one area or another there's so much of  the food world to explore but it was  just haunting me that I hadn't done  something about my Italian heritage and  so I'd been saving ideas for this book  for years just inspired by my travels  all over the country and then of course  dishes that I grew up eating from my  grandmother and great-grandparents and  mom and dad so it all kind of came  together into this you know collection  of recipes that I think are gonna really  inspire American cooks to put something  more fun out when people come over like  not cheese and crackers or chips and dip  but you can put this stuff out and it  can sit at room temperature and just be  a really friendly you know host spread  and it came together in part after  sitting down to this quote little lunch  what's this little well so we had always  talked about our distant relatives in  Italy and it was sort of just in passing  and my family and one year it just my  curiosity got the best of me and I said  to my grandparents like do you think I  could actually go meet our relatives in  Italy you know do you think you could  help me find them and I thought this was  going to take like months of searching  through Italian records or you know  digging them up and my grandfather got  me their phone numbers and addresses the  next day I took a solo trip to Italy and  met some distant cousins and aunts and  uncles and saw my great-grandfather's  house that he came from to the States  and it was so moving the most important  thing I've ever done  and basically my answer that day I had  sort of mediocre Italian and they said  oh we're gonna make you a little lunch  or that's what I thought they said yeah  so I stuffed myself there like veal and  pork meatballs and sausages and eggplant  parmigiana and all this beautiful stuff  and then they were like and here's lunch  and the pasta came out and swordfish and  cake and all this and I sort of had this  aha moment where I thought I could have  just been good with the antipasti like  this could be a trial and so it's how my  husband and I entertain now and along  those lines because it is it's not an  appetizer you can make meals of  appetizers what are some of the other we  see some of them here but what else did  it inspire in you well I think one thing  that American hosts kind of get uptight  about is that you know antipasto or  appetizer  are these like fussy little finger foods  and you're planning so much else in the  kitchen you don't really want to fuss  over anything so the the you know  Italians make everything look easy the  antipasti is the first example of that  because what they do is they just use  what's in season and what's local to  them and what's inexpensive and then  they just you know make it into a  beautiful platter or spread that people  can help themselves as we enjoy this  meal and as you sign the dish if you can  have it with anyone past or present who  would it be has to be my  great-grandparents and guys today is my  grandma Stella's birthday with us but I  know she's here with me happy birthday  she's a sad  Stacey out of mine though thank you  thank you so much it was an honor and  for more on Stacey in the dish head to  our website  here we go at CBS this morning  dot-com
 

